    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">753 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Traditional date of the founding by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulus">Romulus</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">509 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Expulsion of the last king and start of the Republic, by tradition</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">387 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Sack by the Senones under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brennus-3rd-century-bce">Brennus</a> (390 in the traditional chronology)</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">27 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Octavian is named Augustus, and the principate begins</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">410</span><span class="rf-text">Sack by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> under Alaric</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">756</span><span class="rf-text">Pepin gives the Pope the Duchy of Rome, creating the Papal States</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1527</span><span class="rf-text">Sack of Rome by the troops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1870</span><span class="rf-text">Italian troops enter through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porta-pia">Porta Pia</a>, and Rome becomes capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1871</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/romulus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/romulus.jpg" alt="Romulus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/romulus">Romulus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brogi,_Carlo_(1850-1925)_-_n._8226_-_Certosa_di_Pavia_-_Medaglione_sullo_zoccolo_della_facciata.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Carlo Brogi, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Rome stands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazio">Lazio</a> region of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiber-river">Tiber</a>, about 24 km inland from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrrhenian-sea">Tyrrhenian Sea</a>. The original settlement grew on hills facing a ford beside the Tiber Island, the only natural ford of the river in this area. The Rome of the Kings was later said to stand on seven hills: the Aventine, Caelian, Capitoline, Esquiline, Palatine, Quirinal and Viminal.</p><p>Archaeological evidence of human occupation of the area is about 14,000 years old, though a dense layer of later debris obscures the Palaeolithic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> sites. Between the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> and the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> each hill held a village, and on the Capitoline one is attested from the end of the 14th century BCE. There is wide agreement that the city formed gradually by the joining of several villages around the largest, on the Palatine, and that this took place in the mid-8th century BCE. Trade with the Greek colonies of southern Italy, mainly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ischia">Ischia</a> and Cumae, helped the process. The view that Rome was founded deliberately in that century remains a fringe hypothesis.</p><p>Roman tradition told a different story. According to the annalists, the twins <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulus">Romulus</a> and Remus, suckled by a she-wolf, decided to build a city; after an argument Romulus killed his brother and the city took his name, on 21 April 753 BCE, the date later set by Varro. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a> joined this legend to an older one in which the Trojan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneas">Aeneas</a> escaped to Italy, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> records further stories, that Evander led an Arcadian colony or that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> founded the city.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ara-pacis"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ara-pacis.jpg" alt="Ara Pacis" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ara-pacis">Ara Pacis</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ara_Pacis_(SW).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rabax63, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Rome was large for a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> settlement. Most of the bigger Early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latium">Latium</a> covered 50 to 80 hectares, while Rome had reached about 200. By 550 BCE, according to one estimate, it covered about 285 hectares and held 35,000 people, though others put the population just under 100,000 between 600 and 500 BCE. It passed 300,000 by 150 BCE. The victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-punic-war">First Punic War</a> brought its first provinces outside the peninsula, and Rome became the capital of an overseas empire for the first time. In the 2nd century BCE farmers driven from their land by large slave-worked estates moved to the city in great numbers.</p><p>Under Augustus the city was rebuilt on a larger scale. He is said to have remarked that he found Rome a city of brick and left it a city of marble, and he added the Forum of Augustus and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ara-pacis">Ara Pacis</a>. The size of the city in his time is a matter of speculation. Glenn Storey estimates 450,000 people, Neville Morley roughly 800,000 and Whitney Oates 1.2 million, and estimates of its peak range from 450,000 to over 3.5 million, with 1 to 2 million most often favoured. The city was subsidised, with roughly 15 to 25 percent of its grain supply paid for by the state, and it depended on goods and taxes from the provinces. Commerce and industry mattered less than in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>. In 98 CE, according to Frontinus, nine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aqueduct">aqueducts</a> fed 39 monumental fountains and 591 public basins. The population fell after the 2nd century, and when the Aurelian Wall was finished in 273 it was only around 500,000.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/diocletian"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/diocletian.jpg" alt="Diocletian" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Istanbul_Archaeology_Museum_Bust_of_Diocletian,_from_Nikomedia,_Izmit,_end_3rd_century_AD_1536.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dosseman, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>According to tradition seven kings ruled for 243 or 244 years: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulus">Romulus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numa-pompilius">Numa Pompilius</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tullus-hostilius">Tullus Hostilius</a>, Ancus Marcius, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarquinius-priscus">Tarquinius Priscus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/servius-tullius">Servius Tullius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarquinius-superbus">Tarquinius Superbus</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> destroyed much of the city's records, and modern scholars treat the list as of doubtful value, though the last kings may be historical. The last three are said to have been Etruscan, and some historians believe Rome was under Etruscan influence for about a century. In 509 BCE, by tradition, the last king was expelled and an oligarchic republic led by two annual consuls was set up. Rome became the dominant city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latium">Latium</a> and then conquered the peninsula, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-wars">Punic Wars</a> (264 to 146 BCE) made it the leading power of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>.</p><p>Civil wars followed: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a>, then Caesar, who crossed the Rubicon in January 49 BCE. After Caesar's assassination in 44 BCE, Octavian defeated Antony at Actium on 2 September 31 BCE, and in 27 BCE he was named Augustus. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a>'s reforms deprived Rome of its role as administrative capital, and in 330 Constantine made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> a second capital. The seat of the western government moved to Ravenna in 408, though from 450 the emperors mostly lived in Rome. Romulus Augustus was deposed on 4 September 476, and Rome passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odoacer">Odoacer</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a> and, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gothic-war-535-554">Gothic Wars</a>, to the Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>. From 756 the popes held the city as the centre of the Papal States, until Italian troops entered on 20 September 1870 and in 1871 Rome became the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Rome&#8221; cites where it covers Empire, Government:</p><ul><li>Charles Freeman, Egypt, Greece, and Rome : Civilizations of the Ancient Mediterranean (Oxford University Press, 2014)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/minerva"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/minerva.jpg" alt="Minerva" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/minerva">Minerva</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herculaneum_Collegio_degli_Augustali_Ercole_sull%27Olimpo_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AlMare, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The city's early population was a mixture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latins</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruscans">Etruscans</a> and Sabines. The Etruscans left a lasting mark: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> learned temple building from them, and the triad of Juno, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minerva">Minerva</a> and Jupiter may come from the Etruscan Uni, Menrva and Tinia, although Rome was primarily a Latin city and was also influenced by the Greek cities of the south. Religio Romana was the main religion of the city until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, which reached Rome in the 1st century; Peter and Paul were martyred there, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishops</a> of Rome came to be seen by Catholics as successors of Peter. Rome also had mystery cults such as Mithraism. A Jewish community has lived in the city since the end of the Republic. It was based in Trastevere and then, by the order of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-paul-iv">Pope Paul IV</a>, elected in 1555, confined to the Ghetto, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> wore yellow hats or veils and were locked in at night. The Ghetto was abolished in 1870.</p><p>From the 2nd century catacombs were cut in the soft tuff outside the city, because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a> forbade burial within it. They hold Christian, pagan and Jewish burials. In the 7th century officials and churchmen from the eastern empire made the lay aristocracy and the Church leadership largely Greek speaking, and eleven of thirteen popes between 678 and 752 were of Greek or Syrian descent. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> great families such as the Annibaldi, Caetani, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colonna-family">Colonna</a>, Orsini and Conti fought each other from fortresses built over ancient buildings. Stones and marble were stripped from ancient buildings for lime and new churches, a practice that continued until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gauls"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gauls.jpg" alt="Gauls" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dying_gaul.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">antmoose, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brennus-4th-century-bce">Brennus</a> sacked Rome after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-allia">Battle of the Allia</a>, in 387 BCE or, in the traditional chronology, 390. In 64 CE the Great Fire left much of the city destroyed, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> reports that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a> tried to put the blame on the Christians. The Antonine Plague is said to have killed 2,000 people a day. Alaric's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> sacked the city on 24 August 410, Geiseric's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> on 2 June 455 and Ricimer's unpaid troops on 11 July 472. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerome">Jerome</a> wrote that "The City which had taken the whole world was itself taken." The damage may have been overestimated, but from 439 the loss of grain from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> shrank the city. Estimates give 800,000 around 400, 500,000 by 452 and 80,000 to 100,000 around 500, though the figure may be twice that.</p><p>Rome changed hands several times in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gothic-war-535-554">Gothic Wars</a>. It withstood a year-long siege in 537 and 538, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/totila">Totila</a> sacked it on 17 December 546. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narses">Narses</a> took it for good in 552. The drainage systems failed, low districts turned into marsh, malaria developed, and the population may have fallen to 30,000. A flood in 589 and a plague in 590 followed. Rome was sacked by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> in 1084, and in 1527 imperial troops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> killed some 1,000 defenders and pillaged the city for days; only 42 of 189 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swiss-guards">Swiss Guards</a> survived. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">Papacy</a> from 1309 to 1377 the city decayed and held under 20,000 people. The bombing of San Lorenzo on 19 July 1943 caused about 3,000 deaths. The Germans declared Rome an open city on 14 August 1943 and it was liberated on 4 June 1944.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/colosseum"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/colosseum.jpg" alt="Colosseum" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/colosseum">Colosseum</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Colosseo_2020.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FeaturedPics, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Rome is called one of the cradles of Western civilisation and Western Christian culture. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a> influenced many modern legal systems, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> gave rise to the Romance languages, and the Roman alphabet is the writing system used by the most languages. The poet Tibullus is reported to have first called the city the Eternal City, and Caput Mundi, capital of the world, was also used. Because the city shrank so far in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, very significant ancient remains survived in its centre, some abandoned and some still in use, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colosseum">Colosseum</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-forum">Roman Forum</a> and the Pantheon, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocas">Phocas</a> gave to the Pope and which probably escaped destruction as a result.</p><p>Later periods added their own layers. The popes made Rome the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> in the later 15th century and under Julius II, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-x">Leo X</a> and Clement VII; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michelangelo">Michelangelo</a> painted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistine-chapel">Sistine Chapel</a> ceiling, and a new St Peter's replaced Constantine's basilica. Sixtus V and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> popes cut new streets and built fountains such as the Trevi, begun in 1732. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lateran-treaty">Lateran Treaty</a> of 1929 created <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vatican-city">Vatican City</a>, an independent state within the city. Today Rome is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, with about 2.7 million people in the municipality, and the seat of the Italian government and parliament.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/kyrie">Kyrie<span>c. 550</span></a><a href="/songs/salve-regina">Salve Regina<span>c. 1100</span></a><a href="/songs/dies-irae">Dies irae<span>c. 1250</span></a><a href="/songs/va-pensiero">Va, pensiero (Chorus of the Hebrew Slaves)<span>1842</span></a><a href="/songs/canto-degli-italiani">Il Canto degli italiani<span>1847</span></a><a href="/songs/giovinezza">Giovinezza<span>1909</span></a><a href="/songs/faccetta-nera">Faccetta nera<span>1935</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Rome grew from villages on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiber-river">Tiber</a> hills, traditionally founded in 753 BCE, into the capital of a republic and an empire. It shrank through sacks and wars to a small town among ruins, became the seat of the popes and a centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>, and in 1871 the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</em></p>

